Modular chiplet design enables independent chiplet development, but introduces integration deadlocks when assembling deadlock-free chiplets into 2.5D multi-chiplet module (MCM) systems. Existing solutions exhibit limitations: (1) turn-restriction approaches treat all potential deadlock cycles uniformly without distinguishing different deadlock structures; (2) virtual network schemes simply duplicate channel buffers on all routers, resulting in substantial redundancy. These solutions overlook the structural differences among deadlock types, leading to unnecessary routing constraints and redundant hardware. We identify that integration deadlocks manifest as either pure inter-chiplet deadlock or mixed deadlock that involves intra-chiplet traffic. These deadlock types require distinct elimination strategies: regional isolation to break pure inter-chiplet cycles, and forward channels to prevent mixed cycle formation. Based on this observation, we propose RPFC, a router partitioning and forward channel routing framework for 2.5D MCM System. Router partitioning divides chiplet routers into isolated regions, breaking pure inter-chiplet cycles without introducing hardware cost. Routing-aware forward channels are selectively added where mixed cycles actually appear, preventing mixed cycles with minimal overhead. We implement two variants: RPFC-Plus employs standard virtual channel configuration with optimized router placement to maximize performance, while RPFC-Lite uses half the virtual channels to minimize hardware cost. Evaluation on a 64-node 2.5D MCM system using both synthetic traffic and real application workloads demonstrates: RPFC-Plus achieves up to 88.54% saturation throughput improvement and 12.96% execution time reduction with only 6.22% area and 4.14% power overhead. RPFC-Lite achieves 36.89% area and 65.42% power reduction while maintaining comparable execution time with 11.46% saturation throughput improvement.